Functional style — when, and when not.

Lambdas are a tool, not a mandate. Used well, functional style makes code shorter and more declarative. Used everywhere, it makes code cryptic. This closing section is the judgement to carry: reach for functions where they clarify, and stay imperative where a plain loop reads better.

So where does functional style shine? First, transformations and pipelines. "Take these, keep the active ones, get their names, sort them" is a chain of small steps — a filter, map, sort pipeline reads exactly as that sentence. Loops that build a new collection from an old one are the sweet spot, and that's where streams, next module, take over. Second, callbacks and strategies — "run this when done", "sort by this rule", "retry this action" — passing behaviour inline is cleaner than a named class every time. And third, small, pure operations — a lambda that takes its inputs and returns a result, touching nothing else, is easy to read, test, and compose.

Where does imperative code win instead? First, complex control flow — multiple breaks and continues, early returns, interdependent branches. A plain for loop expresses those clearly, whereas forcing them into a functional chain gets contorted. Second, side effects and mutation. If the work is fundamentally doing things — updating several structures, input-output, ordering-sensitive steps — an explicit loop is honest about it; a lambda that mutates external state is fighting the style. Third, checked exceptions — the standard functional interfaces don't declare them, so a body that throws an I-O exception needs an ugly try-catch inside the lambda, which is often a signal to just use a regular loop. And fourth, debugging and stack traces — a stepped-through loop is easier to breakpoint than a deep lambda chain, and its stack traces are more legible. That matters in gnarly code.

Here's the golden rule, whatever you choose: keep lambdas pure where you can. Compute from the inputs, return a result, and avoid reaching out to mutate shared state. A pure lambda is thread-safe, testable in isolation, and composable; an effectful one loses all three. And when a lambda needs to mutate externals, that's the strongest hint that the logic wants to be an ordinary loop — or a computation that returns the new value instead.

And in the end, readability is the tie-breaker. There's no prize for the most functional code — only for the clearest. So ask: which version would the next reader understand faster? Sometimes it's the functional one — users dot stream, filter active, map to name, sorted, to-list, reads beautifully. And sometimes it's the imperative one — when the body is fiddly and effectful, with a continue and a side effect like recording to an audit, a plain loop is more honest. Neither is better; each fits a different job.

So, the through-line to streams. This module built all the pieces — lambdas, method references, the standard interfaces, capture, composition, and higher-order methods. Module seven, streams, assembles them into the tool you'll use most: a declarative pipeline, for exactly the "transform a collection" case where functional style is strongest. Carry this section's judgement into it — streams are superb for data pipelines, and easy to overuse for everything else. Because the skill isn't writing functional code; it's knowing when it makes the program clearer.
